### Phase 0c: (removed — NO run-time reference reads)

dt-rcf does NOT read reference files at run time — not the orchestrator, not the workers. All
DQL is curated INLINE in this skill (Phase 0b bootstrap queries, each worker's "Queries to run",
and the orchestrator confirmation queries), already validated against the dt-obs references AND
the live tenant. This is the speed design: workers are pure executors that fire their inline
queries immediately, in parallel — no 30–50KB reference read gating each worker. References are
consulted only when MAINTAINING the skill (see "Skill Registry — maintenance-time reference map").
Proceed directly from Phase 0b to Phase 1.

---

### Phase 1: Data Collection — SCALE-ADAPTIVE (choose ONE path)

Compute `BLAST_RADIUS` = number of affected services (from `affected_entity_ids` /
`smartscape.affected_entity.ids`). Then pick the execution path — the **inline queries are
identical either way**; only WHO runs them differs:

- **BLAST_RADIUS ≤ 5 services AND a single root entity → DIRECT path (Phase 1-direct).** Default
  for typical single-service incidents. ~5 min faster: skips the 4 heavy subagents entirely.
- **BLAST_RADIUS > 5, or multiple root entities → SUBAGENT path (Phase 1-sub).** Keeps context
  isolation for large raw result sets. (`DIRECT_MAX_SERVICES = 5`, tunable.)

Rationale: 4 Explore subagents cost ~5 min (50–65k tokens each) and exist to keep large raw
results OUT of the orchestrator context. For a small blast radius the results are tiny
(everything is `summarize`-aggregated), so that isolation is pure overhead — run direct.

---

#### Phase 1-direct: Orchestrator runs the inline queries (small blast radius)

The orchestrator runs every worker's inline queries ITSELF, via dtctl, in **2–3 parallel Bash
batches** (independent queries fired together in ONE message — e.g. batch A = W-tracing's 5
queries, batch B = W-services + W-logs, etc.). All queries are compact (`summarize`-aggregated;
`limit 15–30` on the few sample queries), so the orchestrator context stays bounded — no mid-run
compaction. No subagents are dispatched. Then go to Phase 1.5 → 2 → 4. (Use the EXACT query forms
from the W-problems / W-tracing / W-logs / W-services sections below — those are the single source
of truth for the DQL.)

---

#### Phase 1-sub: Parallel Worker Dispatch (large blast radius)

**Dispatch ALL workers in ONE message with multiple Agent calls — true concurrency.** Dispatching
them one-at-a-time (send, wait, send) defeats the architecture and is a top cause of slow runs.
The whole point is that the four workers run AT THE SAME TIME.

Workers P, T, L, S run concurrently as subagents (`subagent_type: Explore`).
Skip W-problems if ANCHOR_TYPE != PROBLEM.

**Speed model (why this is fast now):** workers do NOT read reference files (queries are inline),
so each worker's wall-clock ≈ its parallel query batch, not 30–50KB of reading. By the time you
dispatch, the orchestrator's Phase 0b queries have already warmed/refreshed the on-disk dtctl
token, so workers never block on auth. Expected: bootstrap (parallel) + worker fan-out (parallel)
+ synthesis, not a serial march.

**Worker-failure handling — NO silent serial fallback.** If a worker returns gaps or fails, do
NOT re-run all of its queries yourself inline in the orchestrator thread — that serializes the
work AND pulls raw query output into the orchestrator context (the iteration-F failure that made
runs take >11 min). Instead: **re-dispatch that ONE worker ONCE** (as a fresh Agent call, with a
note on what to fix), and if it still fails, record a `⚠️ gap` for that phase in the report. The
only queries the orchestrator runs itself are Phase 0b bootstrap and the Phase 1.5 Absence-Gate
confirmations.

#### Worker Prompt Template

```
You are a forensic investigation worker for dt-rcf (Dynatrace Root Cause Forensics).

RUN YOUR INLINE QUERIES DIRECTLY — do NOT read any reference files at run time.
The exact DQL is in "QUERIES TO RUN" below and is pre-validated. Issue ALL of your queries in
ONE message (multiple `dtctl query` tool calls, in parallel), then summarize. Do not derive
queries from training knowledge.
FALLBACK (rare): ONLY if a query fails with `FIELD_NOT_FOUND` / `METRIC_DOES_NOT_EXIST` (NOT
empty results, NOT an auth error) may you read the single reference file noted on that query to
correct the field/metric name, then retry.

ENTITY MODEL — CRITICAL (the inline queries already follow this; keep it if you adapt one):
- NEVER use dt.entity.service/host == "..." to filter spans or logs.
  Use: filter dt.smartscape.service in [smartscapeNodes SERVICE | filter id == "ID" | fields id]
- NEVER use classicEntitySelector().
- For process/runtime metrics: scope by `dt.entity.process_group_instance == "{ROOT_PGI}"`
  (a standard metric dimension). `dt.process_group.id` is frequently EMPTY — NEVER filter on it.
- For entity names: getNodeName(dt.smartscape.service) or getNodeName(dt.smartscape.host)

DISPATCH CONTEXT:
{DISPATCH_CONTEXT_JSON}

QUERIES TO RUN:
{QUERY_INSTRUCTIONS_FOR_THIS_WORKER}

RULES:
- PREFLIGHT (first action): run `dtctl query 'fetch spans | limit 1'`. If it errors with an
  auth/connection problem (not a DQL error), return immediately with
  `<gap: dtctl-unavailable: {message}>` and STOP — do NOT emit any "no data / not instrumented"
  findings. (This disambiguates a real capability gap from query bugs; if preflight succeeds,
  dtctl works and any later empty result is about the query, not the tool.)
- Run all independent queries in parallel (multiple tool calls per message).
- Do NOT return raw rows. Internalize; summarize aggressively.
- Cap your PhaseResult at ~4KB. Top-5 of anything, not top-50.
- If a query fails with a SYNTAX/FIELD error, retry ONCE with corrected syntax (check: aliased
  bin not backticked; `timestamp` for logs vs `start_time` for spans; single-quoted dtctl arg).
- AUTH IS THE ORCHESTRATOR'S JOB — workers NEVER run `dtctl auth login`. If a dtctl call
  returns an auth error (`token "..." not found`, 401, expired), do NOT log in and do NOT
  report the data as missing. Return immediately with `<gap: auth>` so the orchestrator can
  re-auth once and re-dispatch you. (Tokens are on disk via DTCTL_TOKEN_STORAGE=file and
  normally auto-refresh, so this is rare.) An auth error is NEVER evidence of "no data".
- ERROR ≠ ABSENCE: a query that ERRORED tells you nothing about whether data exists. Only a
  SUCCESSFUL query returning zero rows is evidence of absence — and if that query was
  scoped/filtered (by process, entity, namespace, status…), CONFIRM with a broader unfiltered
  query before concluding "not present" / "not instrumented" / "no logs". A scoped 0-row
  result usually means the filter was wrong, not that the data is missing.
- PROOF OF ABSENCE (required): if you DO conclude "not present / not instrumented / no logs",
  your PhaseResult MUST include the exact UNFILTERED confirmation query you ran and its zero-row
  result. A claim of absence WITHOUT that proof is invalid — the orchestrator will reject it and
  re-dispatch you. (This is exactly how the old "Node.js not instrumented" / "no logs"
  false-negatives slipped through.)
- Execute DQL via `dtctl query '<dql>'` — **SINGLE QUOTES around the DQL**, always. Double
  quotes let bash interpret `$`, `"`, and especially backticks (command-substitution) inside the
  query, silently mangling it. With single quotes the DQL is passed verbatim. Use `mcp__dynatrace__execute_dql`
  only if the Dynatrace MCP server is confirmed registered (`claude mcp list`).
- NO BACKTICKS in DQL. For binned timeseries, ALIAS the bin (`by:{ ts = bin(start_time, 15m) }
  | sort ts asc`) — never `sort \`bin(...)\``. Re-referencing `bin(start_time)` after summarize
  errors `field start_time doesn't exist`; aliasing also avoids the bash-backtick hazard.

RETURN only this PhaseResult shape (JSON). Nothing else.
{PHASE_RESULT_SCHEMA}
```

