## RULES (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

### Inherited from dt-rca

**Rules 1–16 are identical to dt-rca's RULES — apply unchanged. See
`~/.claude/skills/dt-rca/SKILL.md` "RULES (NON-NEGOTIABLE)".** They cover: no skipped data
collection; Mermaid-in-MD / ASCII-in-PDF; executive audience; Appendix-D query logging;
historical verification; compact diagrams; two-file PDF strategy; clean-mode zero-leaks /
consistency / plausibility; and the DQL backtick / timestamp / count-alias, canonical-link,
and service-ID-for-spans rules.

### New in dt-rcf — forensic engine

17. **Smartscape-native entity model** — NEVER use `fetch dt.entity.*` for SERVICE, HOST, or
    CLOUD_APPLICATION lookups. NEVER use `filter dt.entity.service/host ==` on spans/logs/events.
    Use `dt.smartscape.*` fields, `smartscapeNodes`, `getNodeName()`, `getNodeField()` throughout.
    Exception: `fetch dt.entity.process_group` for JVM flags / softwareTechnologies metadata
    (no Smartscape equivalent — document as legacy query in report).

18. **Sub-skills are DQL authority** — Never hardcode a DQL pattern that exists in a sub-skill
    reference file. The orchestrator reads all required files once in Phase 0c; workers read
    their listed files once as their first action. All metric names, field names, and query
    syntax must come from those files — never from training knowledge or assumption.

19. **`not(dt.davis.is_duplicate)` required** — Apply on ALL `fetch dt.davis.problems` queries.

20. **Failure taxonomy required** — W-tracing must attempt `dt.failure_detection.results[][reason]`
    breakdown on every investigation. HTTP status count alone is insufficient.

21. **Sampling extrapolation required** — When reporting request counts from spans, apply the
    multiplicity formula. Report both raw span count and extrapolated request count.

22. **Exception source priority** — Report exceptions from `span.events[][exception.type]` first
    (span-level, ground truth). Supplement with log-level exceptions from W-logs.

23. **Runtime detection required** — W-services must detect RUNTIME from DispatchContext and load
    the correct reference file before running any runtime metrics queries.

24. **Multi-entry required** — dt-rcf must accept all 5 anchor types. Never refuse to run without
    a Problem ID. ANCHOR_TYPE == PROBLEM is one of five equal entry paths.

25. **Hypothesis graph required** — Phase 2 must produce a ranked hypothesis graph before Davis
    CoPilot. Passing raw phase findings to Davis CoPilot is prohibited.

26. **Evidence chain required** — The report's Evidence Chain section must be present and must
    cite phase + sub-skill source for every finding used in the Root Cause Analysis.

27. **Topology from Smartscape only** — Service dependency maps must be built from `dt.smartscape.*`
    span data (outbound client spans + `getNodeName(dt.smartscape.service)`). Never build topology
    from manually stitched `fetch dt.entity.service | fieldsAdd calls, called_by` queries.

28. **Parallel worker dispatch in single message** — All 4 (or 3) workers MUST be dispatched in
    ONE message with multiple Agent calls, and each worker MUST issue all its inline queries in
    ONE message (multiple `dtctl query` calls). NO run-time reference reads (queries are inline;
    references are maintenance/fallback only). Sequential dispatch — or workers reading 30–50KB of
    references before querying — defeats the architecture and is the main cause of slow runs.

### New in dt-rcf — data integrity & speed

29b. **A worker's empty/error result is never a conclusion.** Workers must attach proof for any
    absence claim; the orchestrator runs the Phase 1.5 Absence Gate (one unfiltered confirmation
    query) before any "absent/not-instrumented/no-logs" enters the report. Scope runtime metrics
    by `dt.entity.process_group_instance == {ROOT_PGI}` (resolved by an explicit bootstrap query),
    never the empty `dt.process_group.id`. (See RULES 36–37.)

### New in dt-rcf — report structure & output formats

29. **Emit the canonical skeleton in order.** Use the Phase 4 section order verbatim; never
    reorder or drop sections. The Executive Summary MUST contain all 5 sub-blocks (Critical
    Findings, Impact Summary table, Business Impact bullets, Immediate Action table, inline
    Event Timeline gantt) — never collapse into prose. Run the Pre-Save Self-Check.
30. **De-duplication — each fact has ONE canonical home.** Full evidence → Evidence Chain only
    (RCA shows a 3–5 row "Decisive Evidence for H1" extract). Status breakdown → Failure
    Taxonomy only (no separate Status Distribution subsection). Recurrence full table → RCA
    only (Exec / Hypothesis table / Appendix A get a one-liner). Entity↔ID↔role map → Topology
    Blast Radius only (Appendix A gets the count). Confidence factors → Hypothesis Rankings
    table only. Never restate the same data across sections.
31. **Header = dt-rca clone.** H1 title + `## Investigation ...` H2 subtitle + `Generated /
    Analyst: Claude {MODEL} / Environment` + one compact anchor/window/phases line. No
    tool-version banner.
32. **Footer = dt-rca clone.** `**Links:**` + `*End of Report*`. No tool-version signature.
33. **Glossary is mandatory (Appendix B).** Appendices are A Investigation Details / B Glossary
    / C AI Capabilities / D Query Exchange Log, in that order.
34. **DQL only in Appendix D** — never in Recommended Resolution or elsewhere in the body.
35. **Filename = `RCF_{ANCHOR_SLUG}_{DATE}[_SANITIZED]`** — NEVER the dt-rca
    `Problem_{ID}_Analysis_Report` name.

### New in dt-rcf — data integrity (a query result is not a conclusion)

36. **dtctl auth: on-disk tokens, lazy login, orchestrator-only.** Tokens are stored on disk
    (`DTCTL_TOKEN_STORAGE=file`, harness env — never the Keychain) and auto-refresh, so runs
    normally need NO login. ONLY the orchestrator ever logs in, and only reactively on
    `token "..." not found` from its own query: `dtctl auth login --plain --safety-level
    readonly` (read-only scopes; `dtctl auth refresh` cannot recover a missing token store).
    Workers NEVER log in — on an auth error they return `<gap: auth>` and the orchestrator
    re-auths once and re-dispatches them. An auth error is a retry, NEVER "no logs / no metrics
    / not instrumented". A 403/scope error (not token-not-found) means readonly was too narrow →
    re-login at `readwrite-mine`; still not "data absent".
37. **Error/empty ≠ absence — verify before concluding.** A query that ERRORED proves nothing
    about whether data exists. A SUCCESSFUL zero-row result proves absence ONLY if the query was
    unscoped; if it was filtered (by process, PGI, entity, namespace, status…), re-run a broader
    query to confirm before writing "not present / not instrumented / no logs". Most scoped
    zero-row results are wrong filters, not missing data. (Both false findings in the
    P-26052332 run came from this: a runtime-metric filter on the empty `dt.process_group.id`,
    and a logs phase that hit an auth error — each was misreported as absence.)

